ation but it does  at least ship some of those fixes so  that somebody else  can integrate them over time  anyone else seen any better examples of  that working actually motivating change  i was just going to say that actually in  one of the organizations that i did work  in we did have a no open source policy  you could in fact be let go from your  your um  your job if you were ever caught  um contributing to open source and i'm  not going to name the organization but  i'm also not going to say that i didn't  give patches to other people that may  have  contributed them on under their own  names but um i'm not suggesting you do  that because there was actually just  recently a court case around that which  was a little bit  scary but anyway what i did want to  mention is that this organization we ran  into exactly that problem the idea that  we had packages that were relatively new  that became dependencies in a rather  large code base that was a multi-million  dollar project and suddenly we were in  the position where what would happen we  would create bug fixes or we would put  new features in there and then we would  put them into an internal repository  within the company that was not shared  anywhere and we ran into the problem  very very quickly where we were out of  sync  with everything that was going on  absolutely everywhere i'll just put it  that way and there really was a  the cost of being able to  contribute that back is nothing right  however the cost of fixing this problem  was huge i mean just an unbelievably  huge problem and so um developer time  once again you know just the cost of  downtime the cost of you know bugs on  production systems all sorts of things  like that because  you know the skills that we're hiring  for within the team are not necessarily  the same skills as library developers  and so having people who are able to  maintain a library have never done it  before is  it's a bad idea i'll just put it that  way and so um for sure i've seen that  happen and so it quickly became very  apparent that we needed to have some  sort of open source policy there was  just no question about it whatsoever and  then i do want to mention also that like  it is from a leadership level i can tell  you that i have these conversations all  the time by the way where i'm talking to  leadership and a lot of times also i get  to be the decision maker so it's very  easy for me to say hey this is our  policy this is what we're going to do  and i can open it up and i can offer  people 20 time and all of these  different things in order to work on  open source not every organization is  going to be okay with that right and you  can give all of these different reasons  you can go on and on and on there's not  one organization that i've worked with  except for that one but you can't do it  in your own time but for sure it it  sometimes can be a very hard sell and  there's all of the problems with you  know the legality you know not wanting  to be associated with a project not  feeling like they have any  responsibility not opening the  floodgates that suddenly they make an  exception for this one bug fix and  suddenly they have this huge  tsunami of projects and responsibilities  that are suddenly on their shoulders um  frankly i think that's a kind of a crazy  idea that suddenly everyone would look  to you to be the developers for  everything  but um  at the same time you know  it depends and i think that this is part  of the thing that i really struggle with  as somebody who did go more in like the  corporate route at least for now is that  the idea that you want to be able to  effect change that's one of the reasons  i wanted to go into that kind of  leadership was that i could affect  change and be the decision maker in that  area  but then also running into roadblocks  there and i think it's important to have  more people in the community that are  moving in that direction to be quite  honest that demand that when they're  taking jobs if they have the skills and  they're the people that people want to  hire that you know that's part of the  deal that you bring in a leader that you  know is going to be  involved in this kind of community and  so um  you know infiltrating from within i  think is also a very good idea because  what i think ends up happening a lot of  times is that we have open source  developers that end up creating their  own consulting companies or they go off  and kind of do their own thing  and um and then it's harder  you know it's harder to affect that  change within an organization but it is  a balance and it's also kind of you know  how  how much can you take like how dogged  can you be  um and uh you know maintaining that  motivation to keep pushing for an agenda  and shall we move to our final thoughts  or final comments absolutely i think uh  yes james and leanny uh closing comments  about uh companies and individuals  getting involved in numb focus  so perhaps we can go and reverse order  liath and ian and katrina than myself  and then conor i can close us out  yeah be happy to go um  yeah so i would just like to invite  everyone to um  get involved i was just getting ready to  add a couple links in in the chat links  to pages on our website that give you  some more information on whether that is  um making a financial contribution  um encouraging your company to make a  financial contribution or become an  unfocused sponsor and if you want to do  that please reach out and we will help  you with those conversations but there  are also other ways to get involved num  focus is really increasing our community  involvement through several committees  and we've got  affiliated project selection committee  where we now have a community-led  committee that is helping  review applications for our affiliated  projects and  through small development grants and  our disk committee  is getting ready to open up nominations  for that involvement that's our  dei initiative so there's lots of ways  to get involved please  go to the website research or if you  want additional information or you're  not finding information um happy to  answer those questions you can reach me  at leah  mfocus.org  for me the wrap-up is easy um one thing  i found  that works quite well is a company's  motivation to hire  so when a company is motivated to hire  and have them think about contributing  which could be a talk at a community  event or a conference or sponsoring a  conference and that's been a strong  motivator in pirate in london with  larger organizations and get them to  point their wallets towards sponsoring  in some shape or form  and then that tends to work pretty well  and that brings of course uh support  into our community uh where it can be  directed to uh long-term support for the  projects so that'll be my closing note  uh katrina  yeah so um  all of that and i just wanted to do this  that um a couple of things i do want to  mention i was just talking to my team  today about getting involved in projects  and that sometimes people find that  barrier to entry a little bit high and  so one of my big suggestions i always  offer people if they do want to get  involved in projects is to make pull  requests on documentation that is a  really good way to get involved because  there's never enough documentation  on on projects and it's a great way to  get  familiar with a project learn the people  who are on the project and start  becoming a part of the community  and that's a very direct way that people  you know contributors can get involved  and then on top of that i do want to  mention that within companies that a lot  of support you'd be surprised what the  voices of people on teams even you know  independent or individual contributors  within organizations pushing up from the  bottom in order to be able to um effect  change and being able to ask for support  and putting people in touch with the  right people so that we can talk about  you know corporate sponsorships and pi  data sponsorships and all those things  um you know all of the uh  offices that we work with in order to  solicit you know donations these people  change all the time or there might be  some other person we can talk to or  there's another you know division we can  talk to and things like that in order to  help track down these funds and then um  the last thing i do want to mention is  the the need i think for us to  acknowledge that we do need money in our  unrestricted funds  that that was one in particular that i  think that we should be pushing right  now as a community because um this does  go you know it's great to earmark funds  for particular projects fantastic it's  wonderful but then all the other  projects and all the other new  initiatives that we're able to fund and  be able to help grow is really important  so  giving directly to num focus to allow us  to distribute those funds is incredibly  important and would be very much  appreciated any way that you can can  help  and my final comment would be that  giving back to the open source community  participating in um focus  is about being a part of the  conversation this can occur at many  scopes in many scales  it can start with giving money to num  focus or donating staff time to num  focus it can be as small as  participating in an event  helping to volunteer for an event it can  be as small as  telling the projects that you use hey  we're using your tool and it's a great  tool  anything that you can do to be a part of  the conversation to be a part of the  open development of these tools helps  benefit these tools and one way or the  other and it is in your best interest to  be a part of them focus in whatever  fashion you choose  either to help be an industry leader and  to lead the direction of these software  tools for your industry  or  to be able to service your own needs for  these projects by letting the project  know i use your tool i love it but could  you also do this or i use your tool and  i have this little fix could you help me  just make sure that this gets in  whatever the case may be there is a  benefit to be had at whatever scale that  you contribute to the open source  community and it really behooves you to  be a bigger part of this and so if you  are interested in any of these ideas and  getting involved in whatever fashion or  getting more involved please feel free  to reach out to me to katrina to ian or  to leah we'll share our contact details  in the zoom  chat  and conrad hopefully we'll also have an  opportunity to share with the attendees  after the fact any final details we have  but otherwise you can always email  believe it's info numfocus.org to reach  the num focus switchboard or tweet at us  at numfocus on twitter  thanks everyone for joining in i'll hand  it over to conrad to close us up  cool thank you um yeah uh just from like  uh  the current macro perspective uh it's  been increasingly hard to find good  talent across the world it's only going  to be increasingly harder  and so i think it's very important for  companies to  be able to provide their staff with  the ability to  the feeling that they're doing something  meaningful and also  just uh provide them with  uh  the ability to stay let's see to keep  the edge right by by contributing to  um open source projects and and being  the most updated with uh all the neos  open source stuff  um so  um thank you everyone uh for joining  uh  i'm sure we all had like a very  interesting convo um and we'll hope we  hope to see you at the rest of the  conference and i'm going to say thank  you again to conrad because it's 5 a.m  for you  you're doing a crazy good job there out  in hong kong thank you very much for  staying up so late to run these uh these  expert briefing sessions today  and for your work on monday running them  as well it's been a great first  experiment i hope we can repeat to these  for future conferences
 

